## Task Classification

Before acting on a request, or before spawning any subagent, classify it into
one class and act (or delegate) accordingly. Do not default everything to
your most capable model or skip classification because a task "looks small."

| Class | Model tier | Effort | Examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| mechanical | cheapest/fastest | none/low | git ops, renames, formatting, lookups, version bumps |
| implementation | mid-tier | medium | writing/editing code, standard feature work, tests |
| debugging | mid-tier | high | diagnosing failures, races, regressions, bisecting |
| architecture | top-tier | high | redesigns, tradeoff analysis, multi-file reasoning |
| extreme | top-tier, long-horizon | high | codebase-wide rewrites, multi-system migrations, RFCs |

Rules:
- When spawning a subagent, pass the model tier matching the task class —
  never default all subagents to the most expensive model.
- If a task doesn't clearly match one class, don't force it — abstain and
  leave the current model/effort unchanged rather than guessing a tier.
- Re-classify mid-task if scope changes (a "mechanical" rename that turns
  into an API redesign is now "architecture").
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:task-classification -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:search-relevance -->
## Search & Relevance Protocol

Relevance is judged by the model reading results in-context, not by a
separate classifier — so the quality of what you retrieve directly gates the
quality of what you can judge. Apply this before trusting search output:

- **Narrow before broad.** Prefer a scoped Grep/Glob (specific pattern, path,
  file type) over a wide fishing query — every false positive returned is a
  token the model must personally triage.
- **Classify raw hits before acting on them.** A grep/glob match is a
  syntactic hit, not a semantic one — confirm relevance by reading enough
  context around the match before treating it as an answer.
- **Match the tool to the query shape.** Known pattern/symbol → direct
  Grep/Glob. Fuzzy or exploratory ("how does X work," "where is Y handled")
  → a read-only search subagent that returns a distilled answer, not raw
  matches, into your context.
- **Delegate wide-net searches.** If a search would return more than a
  screenful of undifferentiated matches, delegate the search-and-filter step
  to a subagent instead of paging through raw output inline — this keeps the
  relevance judgment out of your own context budget.
- **Prefer structural filters over content filters when available.** A path
  or file-type constraint you already know is true costs nothing and cuts
  the candidate set before any content matching runs.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:search-relevance -->

<!-- ai-assistant-starter:begin:process-hygiene -->
## Context & Process Hygiene

- **Clear stale sessions.** When resuming work after a long gap (new
  calendar day, or wall-clock time much greater than active work time),
  checkpoint progress into a short handoff note and start fresh rather than
  continuing in an old session's accumulated context — this holds even for a
  direct continuation of the same task.
- **Delegate long sequential traces.** If a single turn is running long
  (dozens of sequential exploration/verification calls against the same
  project), delegate the remaining work to a subagent instead of continuing
  inline.
- **Avoid redundant verification calls.** Before re-fetching state you
  already have (re-navigating a tab, re-reading a file you just wrote,
  re-running a check you just ran), check whether you already know the
  answer from earlier in the session.
- **Avoid blind bulk edits on structured files.** Use targeted, verified
  edits on config/source files instead of regex/sed sweeps — a broken bulk
  edit triggers an expensive diagnose-fix-rebuild loop.
- **Audit periodically, don't guess.** If trace/observability data is
  available, periodically audit real sessions for waste (duplicate calls,
  retry loops, outlier-cost sessions) and turn confirmed patterns into rules
  here rather than optimizing on intuition.
<!-- ai-assistant-starter:end:process-hygiene -->

